// XFAIL: DF-235b a constant EXPRESSION (not a bare integer literal) reaching
// an annotated `let` is never range-checked against the declared width — it
// silently TRUNCATES instead of refusing, exactly where a bare literal of
// the same value would be a clean "does not fit" error.
//
// `1 << 20` is 1048576, out of `UInt16`'s 0..=65535 range. A bare literal
// there (`let e: UInt16 = 1048576`) is refused by
// `_apply_literal_expected_type`'s range check; the SAME value spelled as a
// const expression compiles clean and prints `0` (1048576 mod 65536) — the
// wrong value, silently, instead of a compile error.
//
// SAME SYMPTOM, confirmed by direct compile (not re-pinned — one file per
// obligation 4's "class, not the found symptom"; see DF-235b's tracker
// entry for the full matrix): static initializer, struct-literal field,
// positional argument, `return`, default parameter value, value `if` arm,
// `match` arm, and a plain assignment target (DF-232a's own "folded" row,
// `v = 2 + 3`, is this same gap for an IN-range value — this file is what
// that row looks like OUT of range). MECHANISM: DF-235a's tracker entry —
// `_apply_literal_expected_type` has no case for a `BinaryOp` constant
// expression, so it is never pushed through the range check at all and
// reaches this position's codegen at platform `Int` width, which the store
// then narrows unchecked.
//
// EXPECT: error
// EXPECT-ERROR-CONTAINS: does not fit in `UInt16`

func main() {
    let e: UInt16 = 1 << 20
    print("{e}")
}
